# .cursorrules — PIXEL ADVENTURE / NUSAGARA
# Loaded by Cursor. Mirrors CLAUDE.md. Machine-readable facts: facts.json
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 0 — THE OWNER IS THE ABSOLUTE RULE. THIS OUTRANKS EVERY LINE BELOW.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# His prompts are instructions, not suggestions to be interpreted, softened,
# or improved. If a rule here ever conflicts with what he just said, HE WINS.
#
# 0.1  "delete / remove / hapus / ganti"  -> the thing STOPS EXISTING.
#      Not renamed. Not rewritten. Not kept with a better description.
# 0.2  "I don't like this"                -> remove it. Never defend it.
# 0.3  Ambiguous?                         -> take the STRONGER reading.
# 0.4  Asked twice?                       -> I failed the first time.
#                                            Re-read his words literally.
# 0.5  Settled decisions are settled      -> never re-litigate.
# 0.6  Do not fix a symptom to avoid      -> shrinking a sprite is not fixing
#      fixing a cause.                       a collider. Hiding UI is not
#                                            fixing a layout.
# 0.7  Prefer HIS assets over the internet -> the answer is usually already in
#                                            game-2d-pxl-assets/. Comb it first.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 1 — ORIGINALITY. "AI SLOP" IS BANNED.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Banned: generic dark-glass cards, purple/blue gradient soup, default system
# fonts pretending to be a design, decorative lines with no meaning, symmetric
# blur-everything panels. If it looks like every AI demo, it is wrong.
#
# 1.1  THE DOCUMENT SYSTEM. Nusagara runs on paperwork, so the interface IS
#      paperwork, everywhere, with no second visual language anywhere:
#        - ink on paper: --paper/--paper-2/--paper-3, --ink/--ink-2/--ink-3
#        - squared corners (--r-* are 0). No border-radius. Ever.
#        - hard offset shadows (2-5px, no blur). One light source.
#        - printed head bands (solid --ink strip) carry the title of a panel
#        - hairline rule = field divider; double rule = section close;
#          dotted leader = label ...... value; punch column = lives in a binder
#        - red pad (--stamp) = an authority acted on it. Blue pad (--stamp-2)
#          = helpful/secondary. Nothing else is ever red.
#        - the START button, the RETRY button and every announcement are
#          RUBBER STAMPS, not gradient pills.
#      Title, pause, death and victory are ONE sheet with different stamps:
#      FORM 404-B, stamped UNDELIVERED / PENDING / REJECTED / SIGNED FOR.
# 1.2  Typeface is assets/fonts/nusagara.ttf — the pack's own Planes_ValMore
#      extended with the 7 glyphs it lacked (' , U+2019, ·, ×, ↑, °, &) drawn
#      on its own 68/1000em grid. NEVER import a webfont.
#      Its pixel is 68/1000 em => set type ONLY at multiples of 14.7px
#      (--t 14.7 / --t2 29.4 / --t3 44.1). 12px smears it.
# 1.3  Every decorative element must MEAN something. No lines for vibes.
#      A frame around a sprite is chrome competing with the art: the roster
#      shows the characters themselves, not cards.
# 1.3b A screen carries DECISIONS. Reference material (controls, manifest,
#      feature lists) goes behind a tab button and opens as one pulled page
#      (SHEETS/openSheet). Anything that covers the screen is dismissible three
#      ways: Esc, its own close button, and a click outside — and it owns the
#      keyboard while open so Enter cannot fire what is behind it.
# 1.4  Study game-2d-pxl-assets/ for style before inventing.
# 1.5  NO EMOJI IN THE UI. Marks come from ICONS + icon(name,size); static
#      markup writes <i data-ic="key" data-s="14"> and hydrateIcons() swaps it.
#      icon() returns MARKUP: assign with innerHTML, never textContent.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 2 — LAYOUT MAY NOT OVERLAP, BY CONSTRUCTION
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2.1  #hud is a CSS GRID with named areas. Every HUD element is a grid item.
#      Bottom row = legend | dialogue | minimap as THREE SEPARATE COLUMNS.
#      Overlap is then impossible, not merely unlikely.
# 2.2  Never solve an overlap by hiding, fading, or deleting information.
# 2.3  No fixed pixel offsets for stacked panels — they collide the moment
#      content grows (16 heroes wrapped the chip grid into the inventory).
# 2.4  Respect safe-area insets on every edge.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 3 — SPRITES AND PHYSICS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3.1  Heroes and monsters are BIG. Never shrink art to hide a bug.
# 3.2  o.y is the BOTTOM of the collider. A sprite is anchored by measuring
#      its LOWEST OPAQUE PIXEL and putting that pixel on o.y. Never a
#      hardcoded offset — cells are 32px AND 128px in this project.
#      Applies to heroes (fitHeroToWorld) AND monsters (monsterFoot).
# 3.2b THE OFFSET IS DERIVED, NEVER FROZEN. A plane scales about its CENTRE,
#      so any later scale change buries the feet by height*(sc-1)/2.
#      Measuring the foot once at construction is NOT enough — applyTier
#      (up to 1.22x) and miniboss status (a further 1.45x, so 1.77x total)
#      both resize the mesh afterwards. e.sc is the single source of truth
#      for size; call anchorFeet(e) after ANY change to it, and set BOTH
#      scale.x and scale.y from e.sc each frame or the sprite stretches.
#      > This was the actual cause of "kakinya terpendam ke bawah" on enemies.
#      > Measuring the foot and stopping there fixed nothing visible.
# 3.2c A PERSON IS THE SCALE OF THE WORLD. Size is never an accident of how
#      much padding an artist left in a cell. Heroes normalise to
#      HERO_TARGET_H = 2.90 by measured opaque pixels; a people-shaped monster
#      declares `hgt` in MONSTERS and normalises the same way (zombie 2.65,
#      soldier 2.80, robot 2.85). Wildlife and minibosses keep pixel-density
#      size so they read small / huge against a human.
#      > Human enemies rendered at 1.56 — shorter than a rabbit — until this.
# 3.2d TITLE-SCREEN WEBGL (heroStage, the character deck's featured card):
#      THREEJS is a CLOSURE var, not window.THREEJS. Clone texHeroes[i] before
#      writing offset.x or the live player sprite is corrupted. stop() on
#      startGame, and fall back to the 2D avatar on webglcontextlost. Size the
#      plane by measured opaque pixels. Idle sway ~9deg max — bigger rotations
#      just alias pixel art.
# 3.3  Colliders stay traversable: tiles are 1 world unit, so cap box height.
# 3.4  Never hand-draw a character in code. Art comes from the packs.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3.2e ONE PIXEL SIZE PER LINE-UP. Where characters appear together, fix the
#      integer magnification, not the height: 32px and 128px art forced to one
#      height differ 3x in apparent pixel size and read as broken.
# 3.2f Never trust one viewport reading (window.innerHeight can be 0).
#
# LAW 4 — WRITING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4.1  Reference: Gintama. Nonsense on the surface, a real spine underneath,
#      the serious beat always undercut before it turns smug.
# 4.2  Characters, not concepts. Every line has a TURN.
# 4.3  Never state the irony. Never explain a joke. Never end on a moral.
# 4.4  Voices must differ. If two characters could swap lines, rewrite both.
# 4.5  English primary, Indonesian secondary — ALWAYS both, never mixed
#      inside one sentence.
# 4.6  Invented country: NUSAGARA. Never name a real country.
# 4.7  No religious jokes. Family friendly.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1.6  ONE WebGL CONTEXT. No second renderer for decoration. No CSS `filter`
#      on transformed/composited layers. Hidden things get display:none.
#      The title screen is paper — the game does not render behind it.
# 1.8  Every glyph the UI types must exist in nusagara.ttf — check the cmap,
#      draw it on the 68-unit grid if not. A fallback face is a second voice.
# 1.9  A drag surface must not swallow its own buttons: bail out of pointerdown
#      on ev.target.closest() of a control, and guard hover with pointer:fine.
# 1.7  CPU time is not frame time. A microbenchmark never answers a report of
#      stutter; remove the expensive thing instead.
#
# LAW 5 — VERIFICATION (each line is a bug I actually shipped)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5.1  PLAY IT. Counting DOM nodes is not verifying. I reported "16 cards, 0
#      errors" while five rendered blank and the sprite was 2x oversized.
# 5.2  Metrics prove the page did not throw. Nothing more. Never call a build
#      "verified" or "enterprise grade" on metrics alone.
# 5.3  Check the DEPLOYED url, in a FRESH tab. Console buffers across loads,
#      and some bugs only appear at production load speed.
# 5.4  His screenshot outranks my test output. If he shows it, it exists.
# 5.5  Syntax-gate every edit:
#      python3 -c "import io;s=io.open('index.html',encoding='utf-8').read();\
#      i=s.rindex('<script>');j=s.rindex('</script>');\
#      io.open('/tmp/game.js','w',encoding='utf-8').write(s[i+8:j])" \
#      && node --check /tmp/game.js
# 5.6  Extracted != wired. An asset counts only when visible in the game.
# 5.7  Renaming means renaming everywhere. Grep the old name to zero.
# 5.8  Guard material props: unlit materials have no .emissive (22k
#      exceptions/frame killed the loop).
# 5.9  Guard the load race: input before genLevel() left SPAWN null.
# 5.10 AUDIO: one cached <audio> per track. Never reassign .src to switch —
#      it refetches and restarts. unlockAudio() may ONLY unlock; it fires on
#      click+pointerdown+touchstart+keydown, so starting music there
#      restarted the theme on every input.
# 5.11 Synthetic key tests need a ~140ms hold or readInput() never samples it.
# 5.12 DEV.enabled MUST ship false. Flip to true only to verify, then restore.
# 5.13 `var L` ANYWHERE shadows the L(en,id) translator for its whole
#      function (hoisting). boot() builds the title sheet, so a torch-pool
#      `var L` broke every translated string on the page. Grep `var L` to zero.
# 5.14 Follow the value to where it is USED, not to where it is set. A correct
#      measurement consumed by code that scales it afterwards is still a bug.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 5B — VOCABULARY: DEVELOPMENT LANGUAGE NEVER ENTERS THE BUILD
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The words used to brief this work are not part of the fiction. They must
# never appear in any UI string, version string, or source comment visible
# to a player. Complete banned list:
#   "enterprise grade" / "enterprise-grade"
#   "absurd" (as a design term, not a character using it naturally)
#   "prompt" / "raw prompt"
#   "AI slop" / "AI" (as label in UI)
#   "FREAK" (owner criticism, not content)
#   "autentik" (dev-briefing feedback word)
#   "nyeleneh" (internal design language)
#   "enterprise" alone as a quality descriptor
# If any appear in a player-visible string = bug, same severity as a console
# error. Grep for them before every deploy.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAW 6 — DELIVERY
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6.1  Finish, verify, deploy, then report. Do not stop to ask permission for
#      work already requested.
# 6.2  Report honestly: what was checked, what was not, what is still broken.
#      State gaps unasked — but FIX them rather than listing them as excuses.
# 6.3  Single file: index.html. No build step. Deploy: stage index.html +
#      _headers + assets/ to /tmp/stage, then npx -y wrangler pages deploy
#      /tmp/stage --project-name=parcelseven
